\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Language:
\f1\b0  
\f2\b Python 3.12+
\f1\b0  (required for FastF1).\uc0\u8232 \u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Framework:
\f1\b0  
\f2\b FastAPI
\f1\b0 . It is significantly faster than Flask/Django and has native support for Asynchronous tasks, which you\'92ll need for fetching heavy telemetry.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Data Processing:
\f1\b0  
\f2\b Pandas
\f1\b0  & 
\f2\b NumPy
\f1\b0  (integrated into FastF1).\uc0\u8232 \u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Caching:
\f1\b0  
\f2\b Redis
\f1\b0 . FastF1 has internal caching, but you should use Redis to store "pre-processed" JSON for your frontend to avoid re-calculating lap deltas every time a user switches drivers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

